Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that uses advanced features like async/await, Promises, destructuring, template literals, and a class with static methods:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
  static async fetchData(url) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
      const data = await response.json();
      return data;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Fetch error: ${error.message}`);
      return null;
    }
  }
}

const processData = async ({ title, body } = {}) => {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      const processed = {
        title: title.toUpperCase(),
        body: body.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9 ]/g, '')
      };
      resolve(processed);
    }, 2000);
  });
};

(async () => {
  const url = 'https: 
  const data = await DataFetcher.fetchData(url);
  if (data) {
    const processedData = await processData(data);
    console.log(`Title: ${processedData.title}`);
    console.log(`Body: ${processedData.body}`);
  }
})();
```

This code defines a `DataFetcher` class with a static method to fetch data from a given URL. It uses `async/await` to handle asynchronous operations and `Promise` to simulate processing time. Destructuring is used to extract `title` and `body` from the fetched data, and template literals are used for logging the processed results.